<p><a name=3D"introduction"></a></p>
<h2 id=3D"introduction">Introduction</h2>
<p>To authenticate with the Document Management API, you need to use the Hy=
land Identity Provider, often known as the "Hyland IdP". This allows some p=
owerful use cases that make logging into API-based applications look and wo=
rk exactly like logging into other Hyland products. The Hyland IdP provides=
 support for the <a href=3D"https://oauth.net/2/">OAuth 2.0</a> and <a href=
=3D"https://openid.net/connect/">OpenID Connect</a> standards. For an integ=
rator or application developer to make the effective use of the Hyland IdP,=
 you'll need to understand your application's architecture.</p>
<p>This document is intended for customers and solution providers writing i=
ntegrations with OnBase. Internal audiences should read the more specific g=
uidance available from the Identity team.</p>
<p><a name=3D"choosing-an-oauth-grant-type"></a></p>
<h2 id=3D"choosing-an-oauth-grant-type">Choosing an OAuth Grant Type</h2>
<p>OAuth 2.0 provides many different grant types, but there are two that we=
 recommend for OnBase use cases:</p>
<p><a name=3D"authorization-code-with-pkce"></a>
<strong>Authorization Code with PKCE:</strong> This is the most common gran=
t type. Use it for any application that a user would log into or would have=
 access to through single sign-on (SSO). This includes all Web and mobile a=
pplications and desktop applications, whether the application is built dire=
ctly to work with OnBase or a third-party application that integrates with =
it.  <a href=3D"https://oauth.net/2/pkce/">PKCE</a> is an acronym of "Proof=
 Key for Code Exchange".  It is an extension to the OAuth 2.0 Authenticatio=
n Code flow that boasts added security (<a href=3D"https://tools.ietf.org/h=
tml/rfc7636">IETF RFC 7636</a>).  Due to the added security the Auth Code f=
low with PKCE is now always preferred over the <a href=3D"https://oauth.net=
/2/grant-types/implicit/">Implicit authentication flow</a> for client appli=
cations.</p>
<p><a name=3D"resource-owner-password"></a>
<strong>Resource Owner Password:</strong> This grant type is used <strong>o=
nly</strong> for trusted services with no UI that always work in the contex=
t of a given user. An example of such a service would be an application tha=
t automatically polls an external system to make updates to OnBase. This gr=
ant type does not support SSO or other advanced authentication features. Th=
is limitation is OK, since it should only be used in backend scenarios wher=
e the credentials are protected in a trusted environment. (If you research =
OAuth flows online, you'll see that this grant type is deprecated in most c=
ases. Indeed, it lacks security and robust features for any scenario that r=
equires a user to log in, which is why we recommend the Auth Code with PKCE=
 grant for all such cases.)</p>
<p><a name=3D"setting-up-the-hyland-idp-for-grant-types"></a></p>
<h2 id=3D"setting-up-the-hyland-idp-for-grant-types">Setting up the Hyland =
IdP for Grant Types</h2>
<p>Depending on which grant types have been chosen there may be additional =
identity provider configuration necessary to support those grant types.  Fo=
r an up to date guide for grant type configurations please refer to the lat=
est version of the Identity and Access Management Services Manual Reference=
 Guide (MRG), which can be found on the Hyland Community's <a href=3D"https=
://community.hyland.com/technical/authentication-and-security/resources">Au=
thentication and Security</a> page.</p>
<p><a name=3D"implementing-the-authentication-flows"></a></p>
<h2 id=3D"implementing-authentication-flows">Implementing Authentication Fl=
ows</h2>
<p>Because OAuth 2.0 is an open and common standard, it's possible to resea=
rch and implement these flows yourself. However, we recommend you take adva=
ntage of one of the many third-party libraries available, which will save y=
ou from having to do most of the boilerplate work and avoid common mistakes=
.  Libraries are available for many platforms=E2=80=93Web, native, mobile=
=E2=80=93and different licenses, either open source or proprietary.  OpenID=
 Connect helpfully offers a <a href=3D"https://openid.net/developers/certif=
ied/">certified list of these libraries</a>, organized by development langu=
ages.</p>
<p><a name=3D"using-the-access-token"></a></p>
<h2 id=3D"using-the-access-token">Using the Access Token</h2>
<p>If the authentication flow has been executed correctly then the Hyland I=
dP will generate and return an access token to the client.  An access token=
 is simply a string that allows applications to make requests on behalf of =
a user.  The token itself may not have credentials or identifying informati=
on in it but the Hyland IdP will recognize it and be capable of validating =
a request based on the token.  When the client then makes future requests d=
irectly to the Document Management API it should also include the access to=
ken.  The Document Management API will then make a request to the Hyland Id=
P to validate the access token.  If the Hyland IdP validates the token succ=
essfully then the Document Management API fulfills the request and passes t=
he response to the client.</p>
<p>The Hyland IdP issues and expects a type of access token called a <a hre=
f=3D"https://oauth.net/2/bearer-tokens/">Bearer token</a>.  When making req=
uests it is necessary for the client to identify the token as a Bearer toke=
n by prepending the string "Bearer " to the access token.  For example if t=
he access token returned by the Hyland IdP was of the form "1234" then the =
client would modify the token to a new value of "Bearer 1234".  All request=
s to the Document Management API should then include an "Authorization" hea=
der containing the modified token.  If the credentials supplied to the Hyla=
nd IdP were correct and the Bearer token was successfully validated then th=
e Document Management API will execute and return the response to the clien=
t.</p>
<p><a name=3D"onbase-sessions-and-session-cookie"></a></p>
<h2 id=3D"onbase-sessions-and-session-cookie">OnBase Sessions and Session C=
ookie</h2>
<p>Simply obtaining an Access Token from the Hyland IdP does not actually g=
enerate an OnBase session or consume any user licenses at that point. Inste=
ad, when the Document Management API authenticates any client request for t=
he first time, it creates an OnBase session with that authentication inform=
ation.  It also consumes a license and creates the cookie <code>Cookie.Sess=
ion.OnBase.Hyland</code>. The cookie is then included on the response given=
 back to the client and is required on subsequent requests to the Document =
Management API so that it can use the existing OnBase session. Every reques=
t without a cookie will create a new OnBase session and may consume an addi=
tional license. In most programming languages, applying the cookie to subse=
quent requests is as simple as making sure to re-use the Http Client object=
 that was used to make the initial request, it will handle applying the coo=
kie received in the response to the subsequent request calls.</p>
<p><a name=3D"heartbeat"></a></p>
<h3 id=3D"heartbeat">Heartbeat</h3>
<p>Each request will attempt to refresh the session and extend its cookie's=
 lifetime by another 5 minutes. If an application is idle for more than 5 m=
inutes, the cookie and session could be lost. In order to avoid this, an ex=
plicit heartbeat call should be made every 5 minutes. To make the call, sim=
ply make a POST request to <code>/session/heartbeat</code> and make sure th=
e Access Token and Cookie mentioned in the previous section are present on =
the request. So if your API Server is installed at <code>https://localhost/=
apiserver</code> then the heartbeat end point would be <code>https://localh=
ost/apiserver/onbase/core/session/heartbeat</code>.</p>
<p><a name=3D"disconnect"></a></p>
<h3 id=3D"disconnect">Disconnect</h3>
<p>Because connecting to OnBase consumes licenses, you need to make sure to=
 disconnect from OnBase when you are finished. To disconnect from OnBase, s=
imply make a POST request to <code>/session/disconnect</code> and make sure=
 the Access Token and Cookie mentioned in the previous section are present =
on the request. So if your API Server is installed at <code>https://localho=
st/apiserver</code> then the disconnect end point would be <code>https://lo=
calhost/apiserver/onbase/core/session/disconnect</code>.</p>
